# Cloudflare Pages response headers.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS. Pages was answering every request — HTML, JavaScript,
# icons, everything — with "Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate".
# Lighthouse reported it as "0 cacheable resources found": a returning reader
# re-checked every single file on every single page view. The bytes usually
# came back as 304s, but each one still cost a round trip to Johannesburg and
# back, and there were dozens per page.
#
# THE RULE THROUGHOUT: how long a file may be cached depends on whether its
# NAME changes when its CONTENTS change.
#
#   A name that changes  -> cache for a year, never revalidate.
#   A name that does not -> cache briefly, and always revalidate.
#
# Getting that backwards is how a site serves a stale page to a reader for a
# year with no way to fix it, so nothing below is cached hard unless its
# filename carries a content hash.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# CONTENT SECURITY POLICY — WHY script-src STILL SAYS 'unsafe-inline'
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# A CSP is only as strong as its weakest directive, and honesty about which one
# that is matters more than the appearance of a long policy.
#
# THE OBSTACLE: these pages carry 213 inline event handlers — onclick="save()"
# and its relatives — written directly in the markup. CSP has no way to allow
# those except 'unsafe-inline'. Nonces do not cover them: a nonce applies to a
# <script> element, and an onclick attribute is not one. Adding a nonce would
# in fact make things WORSE, because a policy containing a nonce causes
# browsers to IGNORE 'unsafe-inline' entirely — every one of those 213 handlers
# would stop working the moment somebody clicked.
#
# 'unsafe-hashes' with a hash per handler is the other theoretical route: 213
# hashes, regenerated whenever any handler changes. That is not a policy
# anybody maintains; it is a policy that gets deleted in six months.
#
# So script-src is honest about where it stands, and EVERYTHING ELSE IS
# ENFORCED — which is where most of the value is anyway:
#
#   connect-src  is the one that matters most here. A stored XSS was found in
#                the admin activity log during this work. Script running on the
#                page could read an admin's token; connect-src is what stops it
#                being SENT anywhere. Exfiltration to an attacker's server is
#                now blocked even when script does run.
#   object-src   'none' — no Flash, no plugin content, a classic XSS route.
#   base-uri     'self' — an injected <base> tag can otherwise repoint every
#                relative URL on the page, including script sources.
#   form-action  'self' — an injected form cannot post credentials elsewhere.
#   frame-ancestors 'none' — clickjacking.
#
# THE PATH TO A STRICT script-src, in order:
#   1. Enable the Pages build (npm run build), which already moves the large
#      inline <script> blocks into hashed files. See OPERATIONS.md.
#   2. Convert the 213 onclick attributes to delegated listeners. The pattern
#      is already used in unplug-magazine.html for story cards.
#   3. Remove 'unsafe-inline' from script-src below.
#
# A Report-Only header alongside carries the STRICT policy and reports to the
# API. Nothing is blocked by it; it collects exactly what step 2 would need to
# fix, so the work can be sized from evidence rather than guessed at.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# PAGES APPLIES EVERY MATCHING RULE, NOT THE FIRST ONE.
#
# This was written the other way round, and the deploy proved it wrong. With a
# Cache-Control on the /* catch-all as well as on the specific paths, sw.js came
# back with all three concatenated:
#
#   Cache-Control: no-cache, no-store, must-revalidate, public, max-age=300,
#                  must-revalidate, public, max-age=0, must-revalidate
#
# A header naming three different max-ages is not a stricter rule or a looser
# one, it is an ambiguous one, and which directive wins is up to the browser.
#
# So the catch-all sets NO Cache-Control at all. Pages already defaults to
# "public, max-age=0, must-revalidate", which is exactly what the HTML should
# have, and every rule below adds a cache policy only where the default is
# wrong. The security headers stay on /* because repeating an identical value
# is harmless.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Build output — hashed filenames, safe to cache forever.
#
# esbuild writes these as <name>-<hash>.js / .css. A change to the source
# produces a different hash and therefore a different URL, so a cached copy can
# never be the wrong one. "immutable" additionally tells the browser not to
# revalidate even when the reader presses reload.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
/assets/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Icons and the web app manifest. Not hashed, but they change perhaps once a
# year, and a day-old icon harms nobody.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
/icons/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=86400
/manifest.webmanifest
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=86400

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The service worker. MUST NOT be cached.
#
# This is the file that controls what every other file does. A stale copy keeps
# serving a stale site and re-installs itself, and there is no way to reach a
# reader stuck on one. Browsers now bypass the HTTP cache for the worker by
# default, but saying so explicitly costs nothing and removes the doubt.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
/sw.js
  Cache-Control: no-cache, no-store, must-revalidate

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THERE IS NO /*.js RULE, and there must not be one.
#
# A five-minute cache for the unhashed shared modules looked like free speed.
# But /*.js also matches /sw.js, and because Pages applies every matching rule,
# the service worker came back asking to be cached for no time and for five
# minutes in the same breath. A service worker is the file that decides what
# every other file does; an ambiguous cache policy on it is the one mistake
# here with no way to reach the affected reader afterwards.
#
# So the unhashed modules simply keep Pages' default: revalidated every time,
# answered with a 304 when unchanged. They stop mattering entirely once the
# build step serves hashed copies from /assets/, which are cached for a year.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Everything else — chiefly the HTML.
#
# Security headers only. The caching is Pages' own default, which is already
# what the HTML needs, and the ETag it sends makes an unchanged page cost a 304
# rather than the whole file.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
/*
  # No Cache-Control here on purpose — see the note at the top of this file.
  # Pages already answers with "public, max-age=0, must-revalidate" by default,
  # which is right for the HTML: the pages are the product, and an edit to a
  # story or a price must be visible now. Adding it here would append it to
  # every rule above and make all of them ambiguous.
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin
  X-Frame-Options: DENY
  Permissions-Policy: camera=(), microphone=(), geolocation=(), payment=(), usb=()
  Strict-Transport-Security: max-age=31536000; includeSubDomains
  Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'self'; script-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline' https://cdn.jsdelivr.net https://unpkg.com https://www.googletagmanager.com; style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline' https://fonts.googleapis.com https://unpkg.com; font-src 'self' https://fonts.gstatic.com data:; img-src 'self' data: blob: https://*.supabase.co https://ui-avatars.com https://tile.openstreetmap.org https://*.tile.openstreetmap.org https://www.googletagmanager.com https://i.ytimg.com; connect-src 'self' https://unplug-ecosystem.onrender.com https://*.supabase.co https://www.google-analytics.com https://region1.google-analytics.com https://tile.openstreetmap.org https://*.tile.openstreetmap.org; frame-src https://www.youtube.com https://youtube.com https://www.youtube-nocookie.com https://www.tiktok.com https://www.instagram.com https://drive.google.com; media-src 'self' https://*.supabase.co blob:; object-src 'none'; base-uri 'self'; form-action 'self'; frame-ancestors 'none'; upgrade-insecure-requests
  Content-Security-Policy-Report-Only: default-src 'self'; script-src 'self' https://cdn.jsdelivr.net https://unpkg.com https://www.googletagmanager.com; style-src 'self' https://fonts.googleapis.com https://unpkg.com; report-uri https://unplug-ecosystem.onrender.com/security/csp-report
